                    briefing_text_r3:       `A standard nobody adopts is a document, not a standard — legitimacy is <strong>co-produced, not authored</strong>. Decide whom to recruit first to anchor credibility: the insurer who walked away (bring them in to co-design what they'll underwrite), the regulator as legitimiser, a single keen developer, or the large manufacturer behind the rival proto-standard. Set your <strong>open-vs-proprietary</strong> stance: open builds adoption and legitimacy but dilutes control; proprietary attracts a powerful backer but <em>turns your open market into one company's toll road</em>. And decide how to converge or out-compete the rival proto-standard without capitulating to capture.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Mobilise capital, prove formation & defend to the funders`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present to the lead funder and the development-bank investment committee. Structure Verdebuild's catalytic capital so it <strong>crowds in</strong> private finance — a first-loss layer that de-risks participation can unlock far more than it spends (the ~1:11 leverage logic). Commit a private-capital figure toward <strong>≥£75M</strong> and show genuine <strong>multi-actor uptake</strong> toward ≥25 participants. The funders will press hard on two traps: <em>mobilising grants and calling it a market</em>, and <em>claiming a market exists on one signed MOU</em>. What evidence shows actors are joining because the institutions you built lowered their risk?`,

