                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_gap_right:     `<strong>That's the binding constraint.</strong> With no shared, insurer-accepted standard, risk can't be priced — which is exactly why the insurer walked three weeks before completion. Fix the institution that's missing, not the awareness that already exists.`,
                    coach_r1_awareness:     `<strong>Awareness isn't the binding constraint.</strong> Developers already believe low-carbon timber is great — and they still can't insure it, finance it or certify it. A campaign tells them what they already know. Which missing institution actually stopped your flagship?`,
                    coach_r1_technology:    `<strong>The technology already works.</strong> Mass timber, low-clinker cement and recycled steel exist and perform — more R&D answers a question nobody is asking. The market is blocked by missing institutions, not immature engineering.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>Price isn't the wall.</strong> With £6.5M you cannot subsidise an entire market into existence, and a subsidy that ends leaves no market behind. The blocker is that risk can't be priced at all without a standard — fix that, not the sticker price.`,
                    coach_r1_promoter:      `<strong>You've defined yourself as a marketing body.</strong> But a campaign promoting timber to people who already want it changes nothing — they still can't insure or finance it. Verdebuild's job is to build the institutions, not the buzz.`,
                    coach_r1_producer:      `<strong>Verdebuild is not a product company.</strong> The moment you start manufacturing and selling, you've become one more competing supplier — not the neutral orchestrator the ecosystem needs to coordinate around a shared standard.`,
                    coach_r1_charity:       `<strong>Pure grant-making absorbs losses, it doesn't build a market.</strong> If you simply fund pilots and eat the risk, you've created an extended pilot, not a self-sustaining market. Catalyse the institutions that let private actors carry the risk themselves.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the binding gap as the missing standard AND saw Verdebuild as a system orchestrator that builds institutions rather than selling product. That is the lens that lets the rest of the strategy land.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>A soft diagnosis breeds a soft plan.</strong> If your team won't rigorously name this as an institutional gap, you'll default to a campaign or a pilot and burn the legitimacy the flagship collapse already dented.`,
                    coach_r2_standardfirst: `<strong>Standard-first is the leverage point.</strong> It is the root of the dependency chain: a shared, insurer-accepted standard is what lets risk be priced, insurance be written and finance follow. Attack it first and the others become possible.`,
                    coach_r2_insurancefirst:`<strong>You're solving the visible symptom.</strong> The insurer withdrew cover for the most concrete of reasons — but they can't underwrite what has no agreed standard. Insurance-first without the standard underneath leaves you negotiating risk you can't define.`,
                    coach_r2_financefirst:  `<strong>Finance can't lead the chain.</strong> Lenders won't fund risk they can't insure, and insurers won't cover what has no standard. Start with finance and you'll have money looking for projects no one will underwrite.`,
                    coach_r2_everything:    `<strong>You've launched everything at once.</strong> A standard, a certifier, an insurance product and a finance facility, all with 14 people and £6.5M. By month four you've spread thin, delivered none and burned the legitimacy you had left. What's the <em>one</em> move that unlocks the others?`,
                    coach_r2_understandard: `<strong>You've underfunded the leverage point.</strong> Starving standard-setting while spreading money elsewhere means the one institution that unlocks the rest never reaches critical mass. Concentrate on the root of the chain.`,
                    coach_r2_subsidy:       `<strong>You're trying to buy a market.</strong> Pouring catalytic money into subsidising materials props up demand only while the money lasts — and £6.5M can't sustain a whole sector. Spend on the institutions that let private capital carry the load instead.`,
                    coach_r2_sequenced:     `<strong>Standard-first, properly sequenced.</strong> You respected the dependency logic — finance follows insurable risk, insurance follows the standard — and concentrated capacity where it has the most leverage. That is systems change, not scattering effort.`,
                    coach_r2_ignorechain:   `<strong>Ignoring the dependency chain wastes effort.</strong> Chasing whichever partner says yes first means you'll sign a financier before risk is insurable, or an insurer before a standard exists — commitments that unravel. Sequence: standard, then insurance, then finance.`,
                    coach_r3_insurer:       `<strong>Bringing the insurer who walked into the room is exactly right.</strong> Co-designing the standard with the actor who must underwrite it means the standard is insurable by construction — and the partner whose exit caused the crisis becomes its most credible backer.`,
                    coach_r3_regulator:     `<strong>The regulator as legitimiser is a strong anchor.</strong> Their endorsement gives the standard public credibility that private actors then trust — legitimacy radiates outward from a recognised authority. Pair them with the insurer to make it both legitimate and insurable.`,
                    coach_r3_developer:     `<strong>One keen developer is a start, not an anchor.</strong> A single signature gives momentum but not legitimacy — it's still one project. The coalition needs the assurance and regulatory side in the room, or the standard stays a niche preference.`,
                    coach_r3_bigmanu:       `<strong>Co-opting the strongest player first is risky.</strong> Lead with the large manufacturer and you tilt the table toward their interests before the open coalition has weight — the fastest route to quiet capture of the very standard you're trying to keep open.`,
                    coach_r3_openinsurer:   `<strong>An open standard, anchored by the insurer who walked.</strong> Open maximises adoption across the ~30 developers and the insurer's co-design makes it underwritable — legitimacy and insurability built together. This is the coalition working as orchestration should.`,
                    coach_r3_hybrid:        `<strong>Open core with governed certification is the sophisticated move.</strong> An open base maximises adoption and legitimacy while a shared governance mark gives the standard teeth and a credible quality signal — adoption without the free-for-all that dilutes it away.`,
                    coach_r3_proprietary:   `<strong>You've handed one company the gatekeys.</strong> Adopting their proprietary standard buys fast momentum and a powerful backer — and turns your open market into their private toll road. What happens to the other 29 developers, and to your mission, when the owner raises the price of entry?`,
                    coach_r3_outcompete:    `<strong>Out-competing can work if your coalition has weight.</strong> Winning enough adopters that the open standard becomes the default is legitimate — but only if you've recruited the assurance and regulatory side first, or you're racing the rival with no anchor.`,
                    coach_r3_capitulate:    `<strong>Capitulating hands the category to the rival.</strong> Letting the proprietary proto-standard win to avoid a fight is exactly the capture you were founded to prevent — the open market becomes one player's, and your mission with it.`,
                    coach_r3_authored:      `<strong>A standard authored without the demand side won't stick.</strong> Write it in-house and present it to the ~30 developers as a finished document, and you'll have a technically perfect standard nobody adopts. Legitimacy is co-produced — who needed to be in the room before you wrote it?`,
                    coach_r4_firstloss:     `<strong>A first-loss layer is how catalytic capital crowds in.</strong> Absorbing the early risk lets private lenders follow into projects they'd otherwise avoid — a small, patient sum unlocks far larger flows. That is the ~1:11 leverage the funders want to see.`,
                    coach_r4_grants:        `<strong>You hit the number with grants.</strong> But the funders asked whether a <em>market</em> is forming, and a market runs on private capital that expects a return. If only philanthropists will fund it, you've built an extended pilot, not a market.`,
                    coach_r4_direct:        `<strong>Lending your own money doesn't crowd anyone in.</strong> Verdebuild's £6.5M deployed directly is a drop against £75M and substitutes for the private finance you need rather than catalysing it. De-risk the private money instead of replacing it.`,
                    coach_r4_multiactor:    `<strong>That's evidence of real formation.</strong> Many actors joining because the standard lowered their risk — not one signature — is the test of a market versus a project. Paired with a first-loss structure that crowds in private capital, this is a fundable case.`,
                    coach_r4_onemou:        `<strong>One MOU is a project, not a market.</strong> Presenting a single anchor developer's signature as proof of a market won't survive the investment committee. Where is the evidence that other actors are joining because your institutions lowered their risk?`,
                    coach_r4_pipeline:      `<strong>A pipeline of non-binding letters is thin proof.</strong> Letters of intent show interest, not formation — they convert only if the institutions behind them actually de-risk participation. The funders will discount soft commitments heavily.`,

