                    insight_formed:         `A market has formed: ≥25 participants joining, ≥£75M of genuine private capital crowded in, and a legitimate standard the coalition co-produced. You built the institutions, not a campaign — and they now let supply, demand, insurance and finance find each other.`,
                    insight_captured:       `Captured: you hit some targets, but the standard ended up proprietary or conceded to the rival. You handed one company the gatekeys and turned the open market you were founded to build into a private toll road.`,
                    insight_onemou:         `One MOU is not a market: the headline rests on a single anchor's signature, not on many actors joining because the institutions lowered their risk. The investment committee sees a project, and the next tranche is at risk.`,
                    insight_short:          `The market did not form: participants and committed private capital fell short of ≥25 and ≥£75M. The category risks stalling into a permanent niche of one-off pilots while a rival proprietary standard captures it. Revisit the gap you attacked first and the coalition you under-built.`,
                    insight_lowlegit:       `Standard legitimacy stayed low — the scarce resource the flagship collapse threatened never recovered. Without co-produced credibility, partners hesitate and the standard fails to reach critical mass.`,
                    insight_grantheavy:     `Your capital is grant-heavy: most of the money mobilised is philanthropic, not private. A market runs on capital that expects a return — this looks like a subsidised pilot, not crowding-in.`,
                    insight_nochain:        `You did not lock the dependency chain (standard → insurance → finance). Commitments made out of order tend to unravel — sequencing leverage points is the spine of systems change.`,
                    insight_lasts:          `This is formation that lasts: participants, legitimacy and private capital moving together, anchored by an open standard the coalition owns. The category coalesces into a market rather than stalling into pilots.`,

                    hint_captured:          `The standard has drifted toward capture. Shift back to an open or governed-open stance and converge the rival into shared governance, or the open market becomes one player's toll road.`,
                    hint_onemou:            `You're resting formation on a single MOU. Build multi-actor uptake — show actors joining because your institutions lowered their risk — or the funders will see a project, not a market.`,
                    hint_legit:             `Standard legitimacy is dangerously low. Co-produce it with the insurer and regulator and widen developer involvement, or the standard never reaches critical mass.`,
                    hint_behind:            `You're behind the ≥25-participant trajectory with little time left. Anchor the coalition with the insurer and regulator and open the standard to draw in the ~30 developers.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — participants, legitimacy and private capital all healthy. Now lock the dependency chain and keep the standard open so the formation survives.`,

